{
  "name": "0049 coast-down decider 200 rpm, 20 min",
  "description": "0049 / 0040 / 0048 COAST-DOWN DECIDER. Settles whether UL's ~0.93 kHz line needs the drive ACTIVELY DRIVING (electro-acoustic magnetostriction, Eskild's hypothesis) or only shaft rotation (mechanical resonance). Design driven by measurement, not assumption: the line is STRONGEST at 200-300 rpm (41x the local floor) and weaker at 500 rpm (28x) and 1000 rpm (18x), so cycling at 200 rpm puts the test where the effect is largest - NOT at 500 rpm as first proposed. Ten cycles of 200 rpm (driven) -> 0 (ramp, then coast) give many sweeps in each state at matched shaft speed, which the archive cannot: its five coast sweeps are n=1 each and spread 1.9x-49.7x, the same spread seen between adjacent sweeps in one state, so they are statistically useless. Four 100 rpm cycles add a driven reference at the low speed the coast ends in. VERDICT: line present driven and ABSENT while coasting at the same shaft speed -> electro-acoustic, the drive is the source. Line present in both, scaling with shaft speed -> mechanical resonance. Analyse by binning sweeps on telem_rpm_meas and telem_vfd_cmd_hz, averaging within bins - never on single sweeps. NOTE the drive ramps down over its 10 s decel time, during which it is still driving; the true coast starts when vfd_cmd_hz reaches 0 with rpm_meas still above zero, which the 13 h run shows happens at 86-225 rpm. Sweeps every 2 s to catch that window.",
